---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const SampleOutput = `
Total seconds: 9265

2 hours, 34 minutes, 25 seconds
`;

const Starter = `let totalSeconds = 9265;

// Your code goes below this line.
`;

const WrongWay = `let hours = totalSeconds / 3600;
console.log(hours);`;

const WrongOutput = `2.573611111111111`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Time Split</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>Objective</h2>

          <p>
            Take a single number of seconds and break it into hours, minutes,
            and seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the crate problem from Activity 1.6 you split a number{" "}
            <i>once</i>. This splits it twice, and the leftover from the first
            split becomes the input to the second. That chaining is the whole
            challenge.
          </p>

          <h4>Skills to Practice</h4>

          <ul>
            <li>Division and the remainder operator together</li>
            <li>Using the result of one calculation as the input to the next</li>
            <li>Operator precedence and when parentheses are required</li>
            <li>Naming intermediate values instead of writing one long expression</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="the-problem">
          <h2>Why You Cannot Just Divide</h2>

          <p>
            There are 3600 seconds in an hour, so the obvious first move is to
            divide. Here is what that gives you:
          </p>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-7">
              <Code
                code={WrongWay}
                frame="code"
                lang="javascript"
                title="The Obvious Attempt"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-5">
              <Code
                code={WrongOutput}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Output"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two point five seven hours is a correct answer to a different
            question. You need <b>2 hours</b> and then you need to know what is
            left over, because that leftover is what becomes minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Activity 1.6 gave you both halves of that:{" "}
            <code>%</code> gives the leftover, and subtracting the leftover
            before dividing gives the whole number.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Tasks</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Create a new program named{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-time-split</b>.
            </li>
            <li>Copy the starter line below into your program.</li>
            <li>Print the total seconds with a label.</li>
            <li>
              Work out the number of whole <b>hours</b>, and store it in a
              variable.
            </li>
            <li>
              Work out how many seconds are <b>left over</b> after those hours
              are removed, and store that in a variable too. You will need it
              twice.
            </li>
            <li>
              From that leftover, work out the whole <b>minutes</b> and the
              remaining <b>seconds</b>.
            </li>
            <li>Print the result on one line, in the format shown below.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Starter}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Time Split"
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Then test it</h5>

          <p>Run your program again with each of these values:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><code>7200</code> &mdash; should be exactly 2 hours, 0 minutes, 0 seconds</li>
            <li><code>59</code> &mdash; should be 0 hours, 0 minutes, 59 seconds</li>
            <li><code>3661</code> &mdash; work out by hand what this should be, then check</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>All three must come out right.</b> A program that works on 9265
            and fails on 59 is a program that got lucky.
          </p>

          <h5>Challenge (optional)</h5>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Add days. A day is 86,400 seconds, and the same pattern extends
              one more level.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            title="Sample Output"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your program file, plus:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>The output you got for all three test values</li>
            <li>
              What <code>3661</code> should have been, worked out by hand,
              written down <i>before</i> you ran it
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Predicting the answer first is not busywork. On the exam you will
            trace code with no way to run it, and this is that skill at its
            smallest.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
